# 0. Document authority and interpretation

This document consolidates the five product-planning areas agreed for The Arena:

1. Product direction and positioning.
2. Navigation and interface structure.
3. Accounts, roles and Houses.
4. Missions.
5. XP, progression, rewards and recognition.

It is the authoritative reference for the intended product.

It must not be interpreted as a description of the current repository. The existing application may contain functionality that differs from this specification, and the repository must be inspected before any implementation decision is made.

## 0.1 Truth and decision classifications

Use these classifications when interpreting requirements and reporting findings.

| Classification | Meaning |
|---|---|
| CONFIRMED | Explicitly established in product planning. |
| AGREED | Explicitly accepted as part of the product direction. |
| BASELINE | Initial operating value selected for the specification; configurable later. |
| PROPOSED | Recommendation not yet explicitly approved. |
| FUTURE | Intentionally outside the initial implementation scope. |
| UNKNOWN | Insufficient information to make a reliable decision. |
| VERIFIED | Confirmed through actual repository inspection or testing. |
| BLOCKED | Cannot be implemented or verified without a dependency or decision. |

Do not silently turn PROPOSED, FUTURE or UNKNOWN information into CONFIRMED requirements.

## 0.2 Core implementation instruction

The specification governs:

- Product purpose and behaviour.
- User journeys.
- Navigation.
- Account and membership relationships.
- Permissions.
- Business rules.
- Mission lifecycle.
- XP accounting.
- Progression.
- Recognition.
- Data relationships.
- MVP requirements.
- Acceptance criteria.

The existing repository governs the technical facts:

